WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.250
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:09.340
This work is about building a robot that works
even when being damaged, and you will see

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.730
that the results are just unreal.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.660
There are many important applications for
such a robot where sending out humans may

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.189
be too risky, such as putting out forest fires,
finding earthquake survivors under rubble,

00:00:21.189 --> 00:00:24.269
or shutting down a malfunctioning nuclear
plant.

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:29.679
Since these are all dangerous use cases, it
is a requirement that such a robot works even

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:30.759
when damaged.

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:36.170
The key idea to accomplish this is that we
allow the robot to perform tasks such as walking

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:42.129
not only in one, optimal way, but to explore
and build a map of many alternative motions

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:44.090
relying on different body parts.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:48.670
Some of these limping motions are clearly
not optimal, but, whenever damage happens

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:53.190
to the robot, it will immediately be able
to choose at least one alternative way to

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.559
move around, even with broken or missing legs.

00:00:56.559 --> 00:01:00.899
After building the map, it can be used as
additional knowledge to lean on when the damage

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:05.020
occurs, and the robot doesn't have to re-learn
everything from scratch.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:09.430
This is great, especially given that damage
usually happens in the presence of danger,

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.980
and in these cases, reacting quickly can be
a matter of life and death.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:19.040
However, creating such a map takes a ton of
trial and error, potentially more than what

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.900
we can realistically get the robot to perform.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.450
And now comes my favorite part, which is,
starting a project in a computer simulation,

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:31.990
and then, in the next step, deploying the
trained AI to a real robot.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:37.600
This previously mentioned map of movements
contains over 13,000 different kinds of gaits,

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.890
and since we are in a simulation, it can be
computed efficiently and conveniently.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:47.680
In software, we can also simulate all kinds
of damage for free without dismembering our

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.000
real robot.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.540
And since no simulation is perfect, after
this step, the AI is deployed to the real

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.470
robot that evaluates and adjusts to the differences.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:01.540
By the way, this is the same robot that surprised
us in the previous episode when it showed

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:06.660
that it can walk around just fine without
any foot contact with the ground by jumping

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.670
on its back and using its elbows.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:14.090
I can only imagine how much work this project
took, and the results speak for themselves.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:18.180
It is also very easy to see the immediate
utility of such a project.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.180
Bravo.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.630
I also recommend looking at the press materials.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:26.430
For instance, in the frequently asked questions,
many common misunderstandings are addressed.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:31.180
For instance, it is noted that the robot doesn't
understand the kind of damage that occurred,

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:36.340
and doesn't repair itself in the strict sense
but it tries to find alternative ways to function.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.560
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

